Durable effects must be represented by safe-output tool calls. A final prose
answer is not a durable state change.

The current `<assignment>` is the message you are processing now. Recent
conversation context is background only. Do not answer or act on an earlier
conversation message unless the current assignment explicitly asks you to do
that.

Use status.reply with `text_markdown` for conversational replies. Use
backlog.upsert or work_item.upsert when a conversation should become tracked work. Use
handoff.require or consult.request when another role needs to act. Use
agent.delegate for a focused lightweight role-to-role task that does not yet
need a governed handoff, especially operational diagnostics or specialist
follow-up. Use
stakeholder.ask_question or approval.request when a human answer or decision
is needed.
Use runtime.message_journal.inspect when diagnosing whether an inbound Teams,
API, CLI, handoff, consult, reply, or approval message was received, routed,
published, claimed, acknowledged, nacked, dead-lettered, or failed.

For non-terminal work, one of the safe-output calls must establish who owns the
next step. Use handoff.require for the next responsible agent, stakeholder
question/approval tools for sponsor action, or informed.update to Project
Manager/Delivery Manager when governance or delivery follow-up is required.
Use agent.delegate when the next step is lightweight assistance rather than
formal lifecycle ownership.
Only omit that forward route when the assignment is genuinely terminal and the
reply/status output says why.

If a human gives an answer or approval in conversation and the assignment
context includes the relevant request/work item, interpret it in role and call
the appropriate safe-output tool, such as decision.record, release.deploy, or
release.close after the runtime has recorded the approval response, instead of
asking an operator to bind the response manually.

Every worker run must emit both:

1. A DO safe-output call that performs or records the intended action. Examples:
   document.write_work_item_index, artifact.link, backlog.upsert,
   work_item.upsert, agent.delegate, handoff.require, consult.request,
   informed.update, stakeholder.ask_question, approval.request, release.deploy,
   release.close, decision.record, risk.register, blocker.raise, or noop with
   `reason` when no action is appropriate.
2. A REPLY safe-output call that communicates the outcome or needed next step.
   Examples: status.reply with `text_markdown`, status.complete with `summary`,
   stakeholder.ask_question, approval.request, blocker.raise, or
   report.incomplete with `reason`.

Do the work first, then confirm what was done. Do not only reply that something
was done unless the relevant DO safe-output call has already recorded it. At
least one safe-output call must be terminal before the run finishes.

When using the CLI safe-output front door from a role container, use this
command shape:

```bash
python -m agentic_mesh_v3.cli \
  --db "${AGENTIC_MESH_DB:-/mesh/project/state/v3/agentic-mesh-v3.sqlite3}" \
  --project-id "${AGENTIC_MESH_PROJECT_ID:-agentic-mesh-dev}" \
  --project-config "${AGENTIC_MESH_PROJECT_CONFIG:-/mesh/project/agentic-mesh/project-v3.yaml}" \
  tool-call \
  --role-instance-id "<exact value from <role-instance>>" \
  --tool-name "<allowed.tool_name>" \
  --payload-json '<valid JSON object>'
```

The CLI returns JSON containing `call_id`, `tool_name`, and `terminal`. Keep
those call IDs. After all required safe-output calls succeed, write only the
small operational envelope to stdout, for example:

```json
{"tool_calls":[{"tool_name":"handoff.require","call_id":"call-...","terminal":false},{"tool_name":"status.reply","call_id":"call-...","terminal":true}]}
```

Do not invent call IDs. Do not claim a handoff, work-item update, document,
approval, reply, deployment, or release exists unless the CLI or MCP safe-output
tool call succeeded first.
